This not only confirms that at least some dinosaurs had feathers, a theory that is already widespread, but also provides details that fossils generally cannot provide, such as color and three-dimensional arrangement.
Scientists say that this animal's plumage was chestnut-brown on the top and light or carotinoid-coloured on the bottom.
The find also provides insights into the evolution of feathers in birds.
Because the dinosaur springs do not have a well-developed shaft called Rachis, but have other features of feathers – barbs and barbules – the researchers concluded that the Rachis was presumably a later evolutionary development than these other traits.
The structure of the feathers suggests that they were not used for flying, but rather for temperature regulation or for heart. The researchers suspected that the sample, although it is the tail of a young dinosaur, shows the plumage of an adult and not the down of a chick.
