In France, voting is traditionally a low-tech experience: Voters isolate themselves in a cabin and put a pre-printed sheet of paper on which the candidate of their choice is registered into an envelope.
After officials confirm the voter's identities, the voter throws the envelope into the ballot box and signs the voter list.
The French electoral law determines the procedure quite strictly.
Since 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent so that voters and observers can testify that there are no envelopes at the beginning of the election and that no envelopes are thrown in except those of the duly counted and authorized voters.
Candidates can send representatives attending each part of the process as witnesses. In the evenining, the votes are counteded by volunteers under strict supervision and according to special procedures.
