The beginning of Taiwan dates back to the 15th century, when European sailors passed there and documented the name of the island as Ilha Formosa or beautiful island.
In 1624, the Dutch East India Company founded a branch in southwest Taiwan, thus initiating a change in the grain cultivation methods of the indigenous peoples and employing Chinese workers on their rice and sugar plantations.
In 1683, armed forces of the Qing dynasty (1644-1912) took control of Taiwan's western and northern coastal regions and in 1885 declared Taiwan the province of the Qing Empire.
In 1895, after defeat in the first Sino-Japanian war (1894-1895), the government of the Qing Dynasty signed the Treaty of Shimonoseki, in which it ceded sovereignty over Taiwan to Japan, which ruled the island until 1945.
