The Cheop pyramid is the only one of the seven wonders of the world that still stands today.
Built in the third century B.C. by the Egyptians, the Cheops pyramid is one of many large pyramid structures built to worship deceased pharaohs.
The Giza plateau or the “Giza necropolis” in the Egyptian valley of the dead includes several pyramids (of which the Great Pyramid is the largest), several small tombs, several temples as well as the large Sphinx.
The great pyramid was built in honour to Pharaoh Cheops and many of the smaller pyramids, tombs and temples were erected in honor of Cheop's wives and family members.
