To understand the Templars, one has to understand the context that led to the establishment of the Order.
The epoch in which the events took place is commonly referred to as the High Middle Ages, an age in European history that spans the 11th, 12th and 13 Centuries (1000–1300 A.D.).
The Early Middle Ages were preceded by the Early Middle Ages, followed by the Late Middle Ages, which consistently ends around 1500.
Technological determinism is a term encompassing a wide range of ideas in practice, from the technology push or the technological imperative to the strict sense that human destiny is determined by an underlying logic associated with scientific laws and their manifestation in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two general notions. On the one hand, that the development of technology itself follows a path that is largely beyond cultural or political influence, and, on the other hand, that technology in turn has “impacts” on societies that are more inherent rather than social.
One can claim, for example, that the passenger car inevitably entails the development of roads.
However, a nationwide road network for only a small number of cars is economically unsurvivable. Therefore, new production methods are being developed to reduce the cost of car ownership.
Widespread automotive ownership also results in a higher number of accidents on the roads, which in turn leads to the invention of new techniques in the healthcare sector to repair damaged bodies.
Romanticism had a strong, culturally deterministic element, drawn from writers such as Goethe, Spruce and Schlegel.
In the context of Romanticism, it is geography that shaped individuals. Over time, customs and cultures arose with reference to this geography and these, since they were in harmony with the place of society, were better than arbitrarily octroyed laws.
Just as Paris is known as today’s fashion capital of today’s world, Constantinople was considered the fashion capital of feudal Europe.
Its fame as the epicenter of luxury began around 400 A.D. and lasted until approximately 1100 A.D.
Its status decreased over the course of the twelfth century, mainly due to the fact that the crusaders had returned with gifts such as silk and spices, which were deemed more valuable than the supply of the Byzantine markets.
At that time, the title of the fashion capital of the world also migrated from Constantinople to Paris.
