Towards the end of the Middle Ages, Western Europe slowly began to develop its own style. One of the most important developments of the times was that, as a result of the Crusades, people began using buttons for fixing clothes.
Demand economy is agriculture that aims to produce just so much food that it meets the needs of the farmer and his/her family.
Subsistence farming is a simple, often organic crop species that uses native seeds in the eco-region and combines them with alternating field economy or other relatively simple techniques to maximise the yield.
In the past, most farmers did subsistence economics and in many developing countries this is still the case.
Subcultures bring together like-minded people who feel neglected by societal norms and enable them to develop a sense of identity.
Subcultures may distinguish themselves from others in terms of age, ethnicity, class, place and/or gender of members.
The characteristics that delineate a subculture as such may be linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual or geographical, or a combination of these factors.
The members of a subculture often signal their affiliation through a distinctive and symbolic style that includes fashion, habits and jargon.
One of the most common methods to illustrate the importance of socialization is to rely on the few unfortunate cases of children that have not been socialized by adults due to neglect, misfortune or intentional abuse during their childhood.
"Such children are called ""wilted" or wild. Some feral children have been held captivated by people (usually their own parents); in some cases the child's neglection was due to the parents' inability to accept the child's significant intellectual or physical limitations."
Wolf children may have witnessed massive child abuse or had traumatic experiences before they've been exposed or run away.
Others are said to have been raised by animals; some are said to have lived alone in the wilderness.
When raised entirely by animals, the wild child exhibits behaviors (within the scope of its physical possibilities) that are almost entirely consistent with those of its specific foster animal, such as its fear or indifference to humans.
While project-based learning should make the learning process easier and more interesting, “scaffolding” goes one step further.
Scaffolding is not a learning method, but rather a help that supports individuals in a novel learning situation, such as using a new computer program or when starting a new project.
Scaffolding can be both virtual and real. In other words, a teacher is a form of scaffolding, but also the little paperclip man in Microsoft Office.
Virtual assistance is built into the software and is intended to scrutinize, suggest and explain work steps that the student alone may not be able to handle.
