Everyone can achieve enlightenment. The only thing that stands in the way of this goal is our own tension and negativity.
Tibetan Buddhism is based on Buddha’s teachings, but has been expanded to include the path of Mahayana’s love and numerous techniques of Indian yoga.
In principle, Tibetan Buddhism is very simple. It consists of Kundalini yoga, meditation and the path of all-encompassing love.
Kundalini yoga awakens the Kundalini energy (enlightening energy) through yoga poses, breathing exercises, mantras and visualizations.
At the center of Tibetan meditation is the god yoga. Through the visualization of different deities, the channels of energy are purified, the chakras are opened and the enlightened consciousness arises.
Germany was a common enemy in the Second World War, resulting in cooperation between the USSR and the U.S. After the end of the war, the clash of systems, the course and the culture led to ensuring that the countries became hostile.
Two years after the end of the war, the former Allies were enemies and the Cold War began.
It should last for the next 40 years and, in reality, be carried out by proxy armies on battlefields from Africa to Asia, Afghanistan, Cuba and many other places.
On September 17, 1939, the Polish defense had already completely collapsed and the only hope consisted in a retreat and a realignment along the Romanian bridgehead.
However, these plans became obsolete almost overnight, when over 800,000 soldiers of the Red Army of the Soviet Union invaded the Belarusian and Ukrainian fronts and created these after they invaded the eastern regions of Poland in violation of the peace treaty of Riga, the Soviet-Polish non-aggression pact and other bilateral and multilateral international treaties.
The use of ships for freight transport is by far the most efficient method of transporting large volumes of people and goods across the oceans.
The navy's task has traditionally been to ensure that a country remains capable of transporting people and goods, while hindering the enemy's ability to transport people and goods.
One of the most significant recent examples of this is the North Atlantic campaign in World War II. The Americans tried to bring workforce and material across the Atlantic Ocean to help Britain.
At the same time, the German navy tried to stop this traffic mainly by submarines.
If the Allies had failed, Germany would probably have conquered Britain just like the rest of Europe.
Goats were apparently domesticated about 10,000 years ago in the Zāgros Mountains in Iran.
Ancient crops and trunks began to keep them as livestock to easily get their milk, fur, flesh and skin.
Domestic goats were usually kept in herds wandering on hills or other pastures. The goats were looked after by goat herders, very similar to the more well-known shepherds; often these were children or adolescents. These grazing processes are still used today.
Already in the 16th century, conveyor tracks were built in England.
Although the conveyor tracks consisted only of parallel wooden boards, they allowed the horses that pulled the wagons to reach higher speeds and to draw greater loads than on the somewhat rougher roads of the time.
Quite early on, thresholds were introduced in order to secure the position of the rails, but gradually the realisation prevailed thating that rails carrying an iron belt are more efficient.
This became common, but the iron caused more wear on the wooden wheels of the wagons.
Finally, the wooden wheels were replaced by iron wheels. In 1767, the first completely iron rails were introduced.
The first known type of locomotion was walking. Man began walking two million years ago with the advent of Homo Erectus (meaning “uprightent man”).
Their predecessors, the Australopithecus, didn't go upright as regularly.
Two-footed species can be found in Australopithecus fossils from 4.2 to 3.9 million years ago, although Sahelanthropus may have been on two legs seven million years ago.
