Many observed rhythms in physiology and behavior are often largely based on the presence of the body’s own cycles and their production by biological clocks.
Behaviors determined by a cycle, which are not only reactions to signs of external cycles, are known in most living things, also in bacteria, fungi, plants and animals.
Biological clocks are self-sustaining oscillators, which even in the absence of external signals carry out cycles for a time.
The Hershey-Chase experiment was one one of significant indications that DNA is a genetic material.
Hershey and Chase used phages or viruses to incorporate their own DNA into a bacterium.
They conducted two experiments, in which they labeled either the DNA of the phage with radioactive phosphorus or a protein in the phage with radioactive sulphur.
Mutations can have a variety of effects, depending on the type of mutation, the significance of the piece of genetic material in question and whether the cells in question are germline cells.
Only mutations in germline cells can be passed on to children, while mutations elsewhere can lead to cell death or cancer.
Natural tourism attracts people who are interested in visiting natural areas to enjoy the countryside, including the wild plants and animals.
Examples of on-site activities include hunting, fishing, photography, birdwatching, visiting parks and learning information about the ecosystem.
An example of this is the visit, the photography and the study of orangutangs in Borneo.
