Everyone participates in society and uses transport systems. Almost everyone complains about the transport systems.x
In developed countries, you will rarely hear similar complaints about water quality or the collapse of bridges.
Why do transport systems lead to such grievances, why do they fail every day? Are traffic engineers simply incompetent? Or is something more fundamental here?
Traffic flow is the examination of the movement of individual drivers and vehicles between two points and the interactions that take place between them.
Unfortunately, it is difficult to study traffic flows, because it is not possible to predict driver behavior with one hundred percent safety.
Luckily, motorists tend to behave reasonably consistently; therefore, traffic flows have a certain consistency and can be presented mathematically roughly.
To better represent the traffic flow, the correlations between the three most important characteristics were defined: (1) flow, (2) density and (3) velocity.
These relationships help in the planning, design and operation of facilities on the roadway.
Insects were the first animals to conquer the sky. Their ability to fly helped them more easily dodge enemies and find food and sexual partners more efficiently.
Most insects can fold back their wings along their bodies.
As a result, they have a wider selection of small shelters in which they can hide from predators.
Today dragonflies and one-day flies are the only insects that can't fold back their wings.
Thousands of years ago, a man named Aristarch claimed that the sun was at the center of the solar system's movements.
Some people thought he was right, but many believed the opposite: that the solar system, including the sun (and even the other stars), was moving around Earth.
This seems reasonable, since it doesn't seem like the earth is moving, does it?
The Amazon is the world's world's second-longest and water-risting river. It runs more than eight times the same as the second-largest stream.
The Amazon is also Earth's widest river, sometimes up to six miles wide.
A total of 20 percent of the water flowing into the oceans across the planet's rivers comes from the Amazon.
The mainstream of the Amazon River is 6387 km (3980 miles) long and absorbs water from thousands of smaller rivers.
