Today's Casablanca was founded by Berb fishermen in the 10th century BC and was used by the Phoenicians, the Romans and the Merinids as a strategic port called Anfa.
The Portugueses destroyed them and rebuilt it under the name Casa Branca, only to abandon them after an earthquake in 1755.
The Moroccan sultan rebuilt the city as Dar el Beida and it was called Casablanca by Spanish merchants who set up trading bases there.
Casablanca is one of the most uninteresting places for shopping all over Morocco.
Around the old medina, it's easy to find places where traditional Moroccan goods such as tagines, ceramics, leather goods, water pipes and a whole spectrum of tinnef are sold, but that's all just for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city of the Democratic Republic of Congo in the far east near Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by the lava of the Nyiragongo volcano that buried most of the city's streets, especially the city center.
While Goma is reasonably certain, every stay outside Goma should be carefully planned to learn the state of fighting in North Kivu province.
The city is also the starting point for the ascension of Nyiragongo Volcano, as well as for some of the cheapest mountain gorilla tracking tours in Africa.
You can use Bodabodas (motorbike taxis) to get around Goma. The usual (local) price is ~500 Congolese francs for the short ride.x
This in combination with its relative inaccessibility has made “Timbuktu” a metaphor for exotic, distant countries.
Although Timbuktu is considered a tourist attraction due to its reputation and has an airport, it is nowadays an impoverished city.
In 1990 it was added to the list of endangered world heritage areas due to the threat of desert sand.
It was one of one of Louis Gate's PBS "Wonders of the African World".
The city stands in stark contrast to the rest of the country's cities because it has an Arab rather than an African flair.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in the north-east of South Africa and borders Mozambique to the east, Simbabwe to the north and the Crocodile River to the south.
The park has an area of 19,500 km2 and is divided into 14 different eco-zones, each of which supports a different wildlife.
It is one of South Africa's main attractions and is considered the figurehead of South African National Parks (SANParks).
Like all South African national parks, the park charges daily protection and entrance fees.
It may also be worthwhile to purchase a wild card that grants access either to selected parks in South Africa or all South African national parks.
Hong Kong Island is the namesake of Hong Kong Territory and focuses on many tourists.
The buildings that make up the Hong Kong skyline have been compared to a dazzling bar chart visible through the presence of Victoria Harbour's waters.
For the best views of Hong Kong you should leave the island and drive to the opposite shore of Kowloon.
The bulk of Hong Kong Island's urban formation is densely crowded on newfound land along the northern coast.
This is the place that the British colonists have embraced. If you're looking for traces of the area's colonial past, it's a good starting point.
The Sundarbans are the world’s largest mangrove belt, stretching over 80 km from Bangladeshi and Indian hinterlands to the coast.
The Sundarbans have been declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The part of the forest that lies within India's national territory is known as Sundarban's National Park.
However, the forests are not just mangrove swamps – they also include some of the last remaining stocks of the mighty jungle that once covered the Ganges plain.
The Sundarbans cover an area of 3,850 km2, of which a third is covered by water and marshes.
The Sundarbans have been a nature reserve since 1966, and it is estimated that 400 Bengali king tigers and about 30,000 spotted deer are now staying there.
Buses leave throughout the day from the cross-district bus station (on the other side of the river), with most buses departing, especially those heading east and Jakar/Bumthang, between 06:30 and 07:30.
Since the buses between districts are often crowded, it’s recommended to buy a ticket a few days in advance.
Most districts are traveled by smale Japanese coaster buses that are cozy and stable.x
Collective taxis provide a quick and convenient way to get to nearby places such as Paro (Nu 150) and Punakha (Nu 200).
The Oyapock River Bridge is a cable-stayed bridge that spans the Oyapock River and connects the cities of Oiapoque in Brazil and Saint-Georges de l'Oyapock in French Guiana.
Its two towers are 83 meters high, its length is 378 meters and it has two lanes with 3.50 m width.
The drive-through height below the bridge is 15 meters. The construction work was completed in August 2011, but the approval for traffic took place only in March 2017.
The bridge is scheduled to begin fully operations in September 2017. Brazil's customs checkpoints are expected to be completed.
The Guaraní were the most important indigenous group that inhabited today's Eastern Paraguay. They lived as semi-nomadic hunters, who also ran subsistence agriculture.
The Chaco region was also home to other groups of native tribes, such as Guaycurú and Payaguá, which survived by hunting, collecting and fishing.
Paraguay, formerly called Greater Province of Western India, emerged in the 16th Century as a result of meeting the Spanish conquerors with native indigenous groups.
Spaniards began the colonization period that lasted for three centuries.
Since the founding of Asunción in 1537 Paraguay has managed to preserve much of its indigenous character and identity.
Argentina are known for their polo team and players being among the best in the world.
The biggest tournament of the year takes place in December on Las Cañita's polo fields.
Smaller tournaments and competitions can also be viewed here at other times of the year.x
Information on competitions and points of sale for polo playing tickets can be found at Asociacion Argentina de Polo.
The official currency of the Falkland Islands is the Falkland Pound (FKP), the value of which is set in line with the value of the British Pound (GBP).
Money can be exchanged in the only bank on the island, which is located in Stanley opposite the FIC West Store.
The British Pound is generally accepted everywhere on the islands and Stanley credit cards. U.S. dollars are also frequently accepted.
Credit cards are unlikely to be accepted on the remote islands, but British and U.S. currency may be accepted; check in advance with the owners about which payment method is accepted.
It is almost impossible to exchange the currency of the Falkland Islands outside the islands itself, so you should exchange the money before leaving the island.x
Since Montevideo is located south of the equator, it's summer there when it's winter in the northern hemisphere and vice versa.
Montevideo is located in the subtropics. Temperatures above +30 °C are common during the summer months.
The winter can be deceptively cool: Temperatures rarely go below freezing, but wind and humidity make it feel colder than what the thermometer indicates.
There are no pronounced rain or dry seasons: the rainfall remains roughly the same throughout the year.
Although many of the animals in the park see the sight of people used to, the wildlife is still wild and should not be feded or disturbed.
According to the instructions of the park authority, visitors should keep at least 100 yards/meters from bears and wolves and 25 yards/meters away from all other wild animals!
No matter how peaceful bisons, wapitis, moose, bears and practically all large animals can work, it can happen that they move on to attack.
Every year, dozens of visitors are injured because they don't keep enough distance. These animals are large, wild and possibly dangerous, so leave enough space for them.
Also, be aware that odors can attract bears and other wildlife, so avoid carrying heavily fragrant foods with you or cooking, and pay attention to a clean storage.
Apia is the capital of Samoa on the island of Upolu and has a population of just under 40,000.
Apia was founded in the 50s of the 18th century and has been the official capital of Samoa since 1959.
