If you want to drive at high latitudes or over mountain passes, you should expect snow, ice and temperatures below freezing.
On icy and snow-covered roads, liability is low and you can't drive as if you're on nude asphalt.
With snow storms, so much snow can fall in a very short time that you can get stuck.
Visibility may also be affected by falling or drifting snow or by condensation or ice on the vehicle windows.
On the other hand, icy and snowy conditions are normal in many countries, and traffic continues for the most part continuously throughout the year.
Safaris are perhaps the biggest tourist attraction in Africa and the highlight for many visitors.
The term safara refers to a country trip in the usual language to observe the overwhelming African wildlife, especially in the savannah.
Some animals, like elephants and giraffes, tend to approach cars closely, and the standard equipment allows for good visibility.
Lions, cheetahs and leopards are often shy and you can observe them better with binoculars.
A foot safari (also called “bush”, “hiking safari” or “footing”) consists of a hike that lasts either a few hours or several days.
