Duty-free shopping is the possibility to buy goods that are exempt from levies and excise duties in certain places.
Travellers whose destination country has high taxation can sometimes save a significant amount of money, especially on products such as alcoholic beverages and tobacco.
The section between Point Marion and Fairmoun on Highway Buffalo-Pittsburgh is arguably the biggest driving challenge, as it frequently leads through remote, untapped terrain.
If you'ren't used to driving on country roads, you'll be mindful: steep gradients, narrow carriageways and sharp curves predominate here.
The specified speed limits are noticeably lower than in the preceding and subsequent sections - usually 35-40 mph (56-64 km/h) - and their strict adherence is even more important than usual.
Yet interestingly, mobile phone coverage here is much better than in many other areas of the route, such as the Pennsylvania Wilds.
German baked goods are quite delicious. In Bavaria they are quite rich and varied, as well as with the southern neighbour Austria.
Fruit pastries are widely used, with apples cooked into pastries throughout the year, using cherries and plums in summer.
Many German baked goods also include almonds, hazelnuts or other nuts. Popular cakes often go particularly well with a cup of strong coffee.
If you want to make small but rich baked goods, then try what is referred to as Berliner, pancakes or doughnuts depending on the region.
A curry is a dish based on herbs and spices, along with either meat or vegetables.
A curry can be either “dry” or “moist” depending on the amount of liquid.
In the interior of northern India and Pakistan, yogurt is commonly used in curries; coconut milk is commonly used in southern India and some other coastal regions of the subcontinent.
With a choice of 17,000 islands, Indonesian food is an umbrella term that includes a wide variety of regional cuisines that can be found across the country.
However, if the term is used without further attributes, it usually refers to the food that originates from the central and eastern parts of the main island of Java.
Javanian cuisine, which is now widespread throughout the archipelago, offers a variety of simply spiced dishes. As flavors, Javanese prefer peanuts, chillies, sugar (particularly Javanic coconut blossom sugar) and a range of aromatic spices.
Stirrups are aids for the feet of the riders hanging down on either side of the saddle.
They give the rider more stability, but there are safety concerns because the rider's feet may be able to get caught in them.
If a rider falls off the horse and catches his foot on the stirrup, he can be dragged along when the horse passes through. To keep this risk as low as possible, a number of safety precautions can be taken.
First of all, most riders wear riding boots with a heel and a smooth, quite narrow sole.
In addition, some saddles, in particular English saddles, have safety brackets, which make it possible for a stirrup belt to fall off the saddle as soon as it is pulled backwards by a falling rider.
The Cochamó Valley – Chile’s main climbing destination, known as Yosemite National Park of South America, with a variety of large granite walls and cliffs.
