In Christianity, Christmas is one of the most important holidays and is considered the birthday of Jesus.
Many of the traditions around the festival have also been adopted by non-believers all over the world by non-believers in Christian and non-Christian countries.
There is the tradition of spending Easter night awake at some vacancy to see the sunrise.
There are, of course, Christian-theological explanations for this tradition, but it could also be a pre-Christian spring and fertility ritual.
More traditional churches often hold an Easter Night's watch on Saturday night, during the Easter weekend, with celebrations of the congregations often beginning at midnight at which the resurrection of Christ is celebrated.
All the animals that originally arrived on the islands came here either by swimming, flying or flooding.
Due to the great distance from the continent, mammals were unable to embark on the journey, making the giant tortoise the main pasture in the Galapagos Islands.
Since the first humans entered the Galapagos Islands, many mammals have been introduced, including goats, horses, rats, cats and dogs.
If you visit the Arctic or antarctic areas in winter, you will experience the polar night, which means that the sun does not rise above the horizon.
This provides a good opportunity to see the Northern Lights as the skies are more or less dark around the clock.
Since the areas are sparsely populated and light pollution is therefore often not a problem, you can also enjoy the starry sky.
The Japanese working culture is more hierarchical and formal than what Westerners may be accustomed to.
Suits are part of common business clothing and colleagues respond with their family names or activity names.
Harmony in the workplace is of crucial importance, with the focus being on group performance rather than praise for individual achievements.
Often, workers have to obtain the consent of their superiors for all decisions they make and they are expected to unconditionally follow the instructions of their bosses.
